+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Precision:** The choice of $p$ affects optimization criteria. $p=\infty$ is sensitive to outliers [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundaries:** The domain $D$ must be compact and contain all points. Partitioning must cover $D$ completely [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Degenerate Input:** If points are collinear or coplanar in higher dimensions, standard spatial splitting may fail. Variants like PCA-based clustering handle this by splitting along the direction of maximum variance [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Approximation Error Bound:** When using $n_{max}$ for optimization, the strict error bound may not be met [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [PCA-based Clustering](5048-pca-based-clustering.md)
-Partitions points by aligning splitting hyperplanes with principal axes of the point distribution. It is chosen when cluster shape quality is critical for surface reconstruction, ensuring splits are orthogonal to the longest axis of the point cloud.
+Uses Principal Component Analysis to define a local coordinate system and splitting plane, ensuring well-shaped clusters by splitting along the direction of maximum variance. Choose when geometric distribution of spatial coordinates is the primary concern.
 
 ### [Adaptive Clustering with Error-Bisector Splitting](6211-adaptive-clustering-with-error-bisector-splitting.md)
-Uses quadratic polynomial approximations to guide subdivision based on approximation residuals. It is chosen for efficiently approximating smooth surfaces with minimal clusters for a given error tolerance, outperforming PCA-based splitting on data with directional curvature.
+Uses quadratic polynomial approximations and error metrics to guide subdivision, producing long, skinny clusters when curvature is directional. Choose when approximating smooth surfaces with minimal clusters for a given error tolerance is critical.
 
 ### [Hierarchical Density-based Clustering](10952-hierarchical-density-based-clustering.md)
-Generates a hierarchy of nested clusters based on density estimates in multi-dimensional feature space. It is chosen for interactive exploration in cluster space, scaling better to high dimensionality than parallel coordinates and providing an explicit cluster tree visualization.
+Generates a hierarchy based on density estimates in multi-dimensional feature space, providing an explicit cluster tree visualization. Choose for interactive exploration in cluster space rather than high-dimensional feature space.
 
 ### Hierarchy Interaction Framework
-A framework enabling real-time interaction with the cluster hierarchy, allowing users to change hierarchy levels, similarity measures, and apply constraints (fixing, splitting, joining) without re-running the algorithm [S2]. It is chosen when intuitive exploration and manual adjustment of the hierarchy are required [S2].
+A framework enabling real-time interaction with the cluster hierarchy, allowing users to change the hierarchy level $k$ to partition elements, change similarity measures, and apply constraints (fixing, splitting, joining clusters) without re-running the entire clustering algorithm from scratch [S2]. It solves the problem of cumbersome threshold tuning and lack of insight into the data set by providing intuitive interaction [S2].
+*   **Steps:**
+    1.  Generate initial hierarchy using a default similarity measure $s$ on initial clustering elements [S2].
+    2.  User interacts by changing level $k$, resulting in a new partition of elements [S2].
+    3.  Apply constraints: Fixing a cluster removes it from the hierarchy so it is not split or joined when the level changes [S2].
+    4.  Apply constraints: Explicit splitting or joining of selected clusters, adjusting the hierarchy accordingly [S2].
+    5.  Visualize only one branch of the hierarchy to focus on one object of interest at a time [S2].
 
 ### Adaptive Clustering Optimization
-Modifications to reduce computational cost from $O(n \log m)$ to expected linear time for large-scale datasets [S1, unofficial]. It is chosen when processing large datasets where standard clustering is too slow, using batch refinement and random point sampling [S1, unofficial].
+Modifications to reduce computational cost of adaptive clustering from $O(n \log m)$ to expected linear time for large-scale datasets [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Steps:**
+    1.  **Refinement Strategy:** Refine all tiles $\tau_k^j$ corresponding to residuals $\varepsilon_k^j$ greater than a specified threshold, rather than refining one by one, allowing exponential growth of clusters with level index $j$ [S1, unofficial].
+    2.  **Residual Estimation:** Estimate residuals $\varepsilon_k^j$ based on a different set of at most $n_{max}$ points. Note that the final approximation error might not exactly satisfy the prescribed error bound [S1, unofficial].
+    3.  **Tile Lookup:** For any data point $p_i$ and level $L_j$, estimate the containing tile in expected constant time. Maintain a list of all points inside a certain tile available in expected linear time. For evenly distributed data, achieve this by regular decomposition of domain $D$ into squares/voxels, recording a list of overlapping tiles for each [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Parameter:** $n_{max}$: Maximum number of points determining an approximating function $\tilde{f}_k^j$. If a tile contains more than $n_{max}$ points, select $n_{max}$ points randomly [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The standard computational cost for both hierarchical Voronoi and BSP-tree-based adaptive clustering is $O(n \log m)$ for $n$ data points and $m$ clusters [S1, unofficial]. Optimized variants reduce this to expected linear time for large-scale datasets [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Standard adaptive clustering approaches (hierarchical Voronoi diagrams, BSP trees) have computational costs of $O(n \log m)$ for $n$ data points and $m$ clusters [S1, unofficial]. With optimization modifications (using $n_{max}$ and batch refinement), the cost reduces to expected linear time for large-scale datasets [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Measured Performance:** The method is efficient for smooth two-manifold geometries and terrain modeling [S1, unofficial]. It allows coarse levels to be computed quickly, making it suitable for progressive visualization [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Does not require a grid structure; supports "top-down" construction; allows for real-time interaction with the hierarchy; efficient for smooth surfaces [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Standard variants may be slow for very large datasets ($O(n \log m)$); the choice of error norm impacts efficiency and quality; residuals might not exactly satisfy prescribed error bounds in optimized variants [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **When to Use:** When dealing with scattered data without connectivity; when multiresolution representation is needed for visualization or compression; when interactive exploration of the data hierarchy is beneficial [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When a regular grid structure is already available and efficient; when strict error bounds are required without approximation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Scaling:** The "top-down" construction ensures that coarse levels of resolution require less computation time than finer levels [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Does not require a grid structure; handles scattered data; allows adaptive refinement based on error; supports real-time interaction via hierarchy frameworks [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Standard implementation is $O(n \log m)$; optimization with $n_{max}$ may violate strict error bounds; requires careful selection of similarity measures and splitting criteria [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **When to use:** When data is scattered and non-grid-based, and a multiresolution hierarchy is needed for visualization or compression [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When strict error bounds must be guaranteed without approximation shortcuts, or when data is already on a regular grid (use grid-based methods instead) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Adaptive clustering was introduced as a general concept for multiresolution modeling, providing a wavelet transform for smooth two-manifold geometries [S1, unofficial]. It was applied to recover topological structures of two-manifold surfaces from scattered data points in 3D space [S1, unofficial]. The method is used in isosurface compression and visualization, terrain modeling, and automating transfer function design for volume rendering by clustering material boundaries [S1, unofficial][S2]. The foundational work is attributed to the author of "Multiresolution modeling for scientific visualization" [S1, unofficial].
+Adaptive clustering was introduced as a general concept for multiresolution modeling, providing a comprehensive wavelet transform for smooth two-manifold geometries [S1, unofficial]. It was applied to recover topological structures of two-manifold surfaces from scattered data points in 3D space [S1, unofficial]. The method is used for isosurface compression and visualization at different levels of resolution, as well as for adaptive image approximation and terrain modeling [S1, unofficial]. It is also used in volume rendering for automating transfer function design by clustering material boundaries [S2].
 
